  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1130 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nHigh clouds will overspread the area in earnest late Monday in \nadvance of an open wave in westerly flow. As this shortwave tracks \nmore southeasterly and moves atop the South Plains after midnight, \nits overall strength is progged to weaken until it resumes \namplifying Tuesday evening across East Texas. Despite this wave's \nweakening trend Monday night, there is still sufficient lift from \nheight falls and a mid-level vort max passing over the South Plains. \nTotal moisture with this wave is less than ideal as we'll be \ndependent on top-down saturation, and much of the Pacific moisture\nwill be wrung out in the higher terrain of New Mexico. Still, \nthere is enough saturated ascent for at least light precip in some\nareas late Monday night into Tuesday morning.\n\nThe biggest obstacle for accumulating precip, including snow, is the \ndepth of sub-cloud drying which will delay top-down moistening. This \ndry layer extends from the surface to nearly 700 mb, or about 7000 \nfeet thick on the Caprock. Such depths are enough to curb precip \namounts from a swift-moving upper trough with diminishing mid-\nlevel moisture. The residence time of best ascent is confined to \nthe pre-dawn hours on Tuesday, which coincides with 2m temps \nfalling to 32 or less in most areas. Some of this cooling will be \naccelerated by wet-bulbing through the aforementioned dry layer. \nVertical temp profiles point to primarily light snow showers \nbeginning overnight, with a rain/snow mix in our far southern \ncounties toward daybreak. Ignoring the earlier runs of the RRFS \nand REFS and their displaced ascent well south of the mid-level \nvort max, this forecast maintains a global model blend that favors\n30-40 PoPs in our far northwest counties and lesser PoPs elsewhere.\nAnywhere from a dusting of snow to localized 0.5\" pockets on the \nCaprock seem reasonable, but accumulations on paved surfaces may \nprove hard to come by with marginal air temps near or just below \nfreezing and much milder surface temps. With borderline temps and \ntheir potential to keep paved surface more wet than snowy, along \nwith sub-likely PoPs and the spotty accumulation potential, this \nevent is not (yet) rising to meet winter weather advisory potential.\nIf this were the middle of winter, this AFD might only need a few\nsentences about this event, but since this is the first potential\nsnow of the season, it attracts more interest than usual!\n\nSubsidence deepens from W-E by late Tuesday morning on the rear of \nthe shortwave trough. This rids us of lingering PoPs while \nintroducing a push of cooler surface ridging through the afternoon \nand evening. Fortunately, this chilly ridge exits before daybreak \nWednesday allowing light southwest breezes and milder temps. Am \nconcerned the NBM is suffering an unusual 10-15 degree cold bias for \nThanksgiving given multiple MOS and deterministic runs painting \nhighs in the 50s and 60s - all of which have light S-SE flow and \nmostly sunny skies. Would not be surprised to see the NBM trend \nmilder in the coming days for Thanksgiving Day. Dry and uneventful \nnorthwest flow aloft rounds out the holiday shopping weekend with \nhighs finally returning closer to normal.",
